  "title": "ada — there is, there are",
  "pattern": "Ada …? · Tidak ada … · … ada di …",
  "explanation": "Ada says that something exists or is available: Ada air? is 'Is there any water?' or 'Do you have water?', and the answer is Ada or Tidak ada. It also says where someone or something is: Ibu ada di rumah? (Is your mother at home?), or just Pak Budi ada? (Is Mr Budi in?). Where English starts with 'there is', Indonesian starts with ada or with the place: Ada banyak turis di Bali, or Di Bali ada banyak turis. Tidak ada, nggak ada in casual speech, means 'there isn't any' or 'not here'.",
  "natural": "In shops and warungs, Ada …? is the easy way to ask for anything: Ada es teh? Ada kamar? If the answer is Nggak ada or Habis, they've run out.",
  "stiff": "Ada isn't 'is' before a describing word: Saya ada capek is wrong (just Saya capek). And for what you own, the word is punya (the next lesson): Saya punya mobil (I've got a car).",
